    "<p>A <strong>random variable</strong> is a function $X:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$ for which the sets $\\{X\\le x\\}$ are events. Its <strong>(cumulative) distribution function</strong>\n       $F(x)=P(X\\le x)$ is non-decreasing, right-continuous, $\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}F(x)=0$, $\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}F(x)=1$ and $P(a\\lt X\\le b)=F(b)-F(a)$.</p>\n       <p>A variable is <strong>discrete</strong> if it takes finitely or countably many values $x_1, x_2, \\dots$. It is described by the <strong>probability mass function</strong>\n       $p_i=P(X=x_i)$, where $p_i\\ge 0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$. The distribution function is a step function: at the point $x_i$ it has a jump of size $p_i$.</p>",
