    "<p><strong>Geometric distribution</strong>: $X$ is the number of the trial with the first success in independent trials with success probability $p$.\n       $$P(X=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p,\\quad k=1, 2, \\dots;\\qquad E(X)=\\frac{1}{p},\\quad D(X)=\\frac{1-p}{p^2},\\quad P(X&gt;k)=(1-p)^k.$$\n       It is <em>memoryless</em>: $P(X&gt;m+k\\mid X&gt;m)=P(X&gt;k)$. (Sometimes the distribution of the number of failures before the first success, $Y=X-1$, is called geometric.)</p>",
    "<p><strong>Poisson distribution</strong> $Po(\\lambda)$, $\\lambda&gt;0$: $P(X=k)=e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}$, $k=0, 1, 2, \\dots$; $E(X)=D(X)=\\lambda$.\n       It describes the number of rare independent events in a fixed time or on a fixed area; for an interval twice as long, $\\lambda$ doubles.\n       The sum of independent $Po(\\lambda_1)$ and $Po(\\lambda_2)$ has the distribution $Po(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.</p>\n       <p><strong>Poisson limit theorem:</strong> if $n\\to\\infty$ and $p_n\\to 0$ so that $np_n\\to\\lambda$, then $\\binom{n}{k}p_n^k(1-p_n)^{n-k}\\to e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}$.\n       In practice: for large $n$ and small $p$ (e.g. $n\\ge 30$, $p\\le 0.1$), $Bi(n; p)\\approx Po(np)$.</p>",
